<http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_3779268> a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Major Luo-Connecting Point of the Spleen" ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Edited by C. Xinnong, Rev. Ed., Foreign Languages Press, Beijing, China, 1999. Ch. 6, An Introduction to Acupunture Points, Sec. III, Specific Points. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7." ;
    dcterms:description "The Major Luo-Connecting Point of the Spleen is a Major Luo-Connecting Point. Dabao (SP 21) is the Major Luo-Connecting Point of the Spleen. Each of twelve main meridians has, on the limbs, a Luo-Connecting Point to link its exteriorly-interiorly related meridian. Each of the Governor and Conception vessels, and the Major Collateral of the spleen has its Luo-Connecting Point on the trunk. A Luo-Connecting Point is used to treat disorders involving the two exteriorly-interiorly related meridians and those in the area supplied by the two meridians." ;
    tara-ap:hasSpecialPointRole <http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_2392868> ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ a owl:Class ;
            owl:intersectionOf ( <http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_7819406> [ a owl:Restriction ;
                        owl:onProperty tara-op:hasSpecialPointRole ;
                        owl:someValuesFrom <http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_2392868> ] ) ] .

<http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_3815917> a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Lower He-Sea Point of the Stomach" ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Edited by C. Xinnong, Rev. Ed., Foreign Languages Press, Beijing, China, 1999. Ch. 6, An Introduction to Acupunture Points, Sec. III, Specific Points. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7." ;
    dcterms:description "The Lower He-Sea Point of the the Stomach meridian. One of the five Shu Points, the He-Sea point signifies the confluence of rivers in the sea, where the meridian qi is the most flourishing. In addition, each of the six fu organs has another He-Sea point in the three yang meridians of the foot, known as the Lower He-Sea point. The Lower He-Sea points are mostly employed to treat the disorders of the six fu organs in clinic." ;
    tara-ap:hasSpecialPointRole <http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_6392235> ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ a owl:Class ;
            owl:intersectionOf ( <http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_4830205> [ a owl:Restriction ;
                        owl:onProperty tara-op:hasSpecialPointRole ;
                        owl:someValuesFrom <http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_6392235> ] ) ] .

<http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_4105303> a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Influential Point of Pulse and Vessels" ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Edited by C. Xinnong, Rev. Ed., Foreign Languages Press, Beijing, China, 1999. Ch. 6, An Introduction to Acupunture Points, Sec. III, Specific Points. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7." ;
    dcterms:description "One of the eight influential points, the Influential Point of Pulse and Vessels can be employed to treat disorders of vessels. Taiyuan (LU 9) is the Influential Point of Pulse and Vessels." ;
    tara-ap:hasSpecialPointRole <http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_2238176> ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ a owl:Class ;
            owl:intersectionOf ( <http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_7010544> [ a owl:Restriction ;
                        owl:onProperty tara-op:hasSpecialPointRole ;
                        owl:someValuesFrom <http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_2238176> ] ) ] .

<http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_4192840> a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Front-Mu Point of the Gallbladder" ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Edited by C. Xinnong, Rev. Ed., Foreign Languages Press, Beijing, China, 1999. Ch. 6, An Introduction to Acupunture Points, Sec. III, Specific Points. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7." ;
    dcterms:description "The Front-Mu Point of the Gallbladder meridian. Front-Mu points are those points on the chest and abdomen where the qi of the respective zang-fu organs is infused and converged. Located close to their corresponding zang-fu organs, the Front-Mu points play a significant role in the diagnosis and treatment of the disorders of the internal organs." ;
    tara-ap:hasSpecialPointRole <http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_9940480> ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ a owl:Class ;
            owl:intersectionOf ( <http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_8746709> [ a owl:Restriction ;
                        owl:onProperty tara-op:hasSpecialPointRole ;
                        owl:someValuesFrom <http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_9940480> ] ) ] .

<http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_4230489> a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Front-Mu Point of the Stomach" ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Edited by C. Xinnong, Rev. Ed., Foreign Languages Press, Beijing, China, 1999. Ch. 6, An Introduction to Acupunture Points, Sec. III, Specific Points. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7." ;
    dcterms:description "The Front-Mu Point of the Stomach meridian. Front-Mu points are those points on the chest and abdomen where the qi of the respective zang-fu organs is infused and converged. Located close to their corresponding zang-fu organs, the Front-Mu points play a significant role in the diagnosis and treatment of the disorders of the internal organs." ;
