<http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_7010544> a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Influential Point" ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Edited by C. Xinnong, Rev. Ed., Foreign Languages Press, Beijing, China, 1999. Ch. 6, An Introduction to Acupunture Points, Sec. III, Specific Points. Ch. 16, General Introduction to Acupuncture Treatment, Page 392. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7." ;
    dcterms:description "Represents one of the Eight lnfluential Points. The Eight Influential Points refer to the specific points which have particular effects in treatment of disorders relating to the zang-fu organs, qi, blood, tendon, pulse and vessels, bone and marrow. Each of the Eight Influential Points overlaps the other point. It is said in Classic on Medical Problems, \"Zhongwan (CV 12) is the Influential Point of the fu organs; Zhangmen (LR 13) is the Influential Point of the zang organs; Yanglingquan (GB 34) is the Influential Point of tendons; Xuanzhong (GB 39) is the Influential Point of marrow; Geshu (BL 17) is the Influential Point of blood; Dazhu (BL 11) is the Influential Point of bone; Taiyuan (LU 9) is the Influential Point of pulse and vessels; and Tanzhong (CV 17) is the Influential Point of Qi." ;
    tara-ap:hasSpecialPointRole <http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_4235924> ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ a owl:Class ;
            owl:intersectionOf ( <http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_7852472> [ a owl:Restriction ;
                        owl:onProperty tara-op:hasSpecialPointRole ;
                        owl:someValuesFrom <http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_4235924> ] ) ] .

<http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_7226516> a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Acupuncture Meridian"@en ;
    IAO:0000115 "A one-dimensional continuant fiat boundary, demarcated by convention within traditional Chinese medicine theory rather than by any natural physical discontinuity in the body, along which Acupoints are located."@en ;
    IAO:0000116 "Acupuncture Meridian is modeled as a subclass of 'one-dimensional continuant fiat boundary' (BFO:0000009): a TCM meridian is a channel/line demarcated by convention within acupuncture theory, not by any natural physical discontinuity, matching the fiat-line character of BFO:0000009 class's own examples (e.g. the Equator, lines of latitude/longitude)."@en ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Maurer, N., Nissel, H., Egerbacher, M., Gornik, E., Schuller, P., & Traxler, H. (2019). Anatomical evidence of acupuncture meridians in the human extracellular matrix: results from a macroscopic and microscopic interdisciplinary multicentre study on human corpses. Evidence-Based Complementary and Alternative Medicine, 2019. https://doi.org/10.1155/2019/6976892" ;
    dcterms:description "In traditional Chinese Medicine (TCM), meridians are strings connecting acupuncture points, which are considered as passageways through which energy flows throughout the body. The meridian system is composed of 12 principal meridians, each of which connects to an organ system and extends to an extremity and eight collaterals."@en ;
    tara-ap:hasSynonym "Acupuncture Channel",
        "Acupuncture Pathway",
        "Meridian" .

<http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_9493899> a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Confluent Point" ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Chinese Acupuncture and Moxibustion. Edited by C. Xinnong, Rev. Ed., Foreign Languages Press, Beijing, China, 1999. Ch. 6, An Introduction to Acupunture Points, Sec. III, Specific Points. ISBN 978-7-119-01758-7." ;
    dcterms:description "Confluent Points refer to the eight points on the limbs where the main meridians communicate with the eight extra meridians. They are Neiguan (PC 6). Gongsun (SP 4), Houxi (SJ 3), Shenmai (BL 62). Waiguan (TE 5), Zulinqi (GB 41 ), Lieque (LU 7) and Zhaohai (KI 6), which are respectively connected with the Yin Link Vessel. Thoroughfare Vessel, governor Vessel, Yang Heel Vessel. Yang Link Vessel, Belt Vessel, Conception Vessel and Yin Heel Vessel. The Eight Confluent Points are used to treat a variety of disorders of the corresponding eight extra meridians." ;
    tara-ap:hasSpecialPointRole <http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_3091322> ;
    owl:equivalentClass [ a owl:Class ;
            owl:intersectionOf ( <http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_4943316> [ a owl:Restriction ;
                        owl:onProperty tara-op:hasSpecialPointRole ;
                        owl:someValuesFrom <http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_3091322> ] ) ] .

tara-ap:hasInnervationInformation a owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "Innervation Information"@en ;
    dcterms:description """An annotation property that describes the nerve supply in the vicinity of an acupoint relevant to safe needling. 

Example: 'LU 1' tara:hasInnervationDescription "The intermediate supraclavicular nerve, the branches of the anterior thoracic nerve, and the lateral cutaneous branch of the first intercostal nerve.\""""@en ;
    dcterms:isRequiredBy tara-ap:hasRelatedInnervation,
        tara-ap:hasRelatedNerve,
        tara-op:hasRelatedInnervation,
        tara-op:hasRelatedNerve ;
    rdfs:domain <http://www.acupunctureresearch.org/tara/ontology/TARA_1132428> ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf tara-ap:hasAcupointInformation .

